---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
const updated = "25 October 2026";
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="What Blueprint Deck Kit, its decks and this website collect: nothing about you. The few requests that happen are listed here.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-brand">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="prose mt-10">
      <p>
        Blueprint Deck Kit is free, open-source software (MIT) that runs on your own computer or in your own AI app.
        It has no accounts, no servers that receive your content, no analytics and no tracking. This page lists every
        place where data moves, so you can check it.
      </p>

      <h2>The skill (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, the chat apps and other agents)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It reads only the files and web pages that you or your config name, and writes the deck and its report into your project. Your agent processes them under its own terms (for example Anthropic's, OpenAI's or Google's); the skill sends them nowhere else.</li>
        <li>Once per run it may ask GitHub's public API for the latest release (<code>api.github.com/repos/lukasvecerka23/blueprint-deck-kit/releases/latest</code>), to tell you when a newer version is out. That is a plain request with no credentials and none of your content; GitHub sees it like any other visit.</li>
        <li>If you set <code>branding.website</code>, it reads that page to take your logo and colours.</li>
        <li>Optional checks (<code>tools/check.mjs</code>, <code>tools/insert.mjs</code>) run locally with Node and send nothing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The decks it makes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A deck is one HTML file. It makes no network requests at all: a Content Security Policy in the file blocks them, and the engine sanitises its content before showing it.</li>
        <li>Your browser keeps unsaved edits and a few preferences (such as whether you've seen the editor's introduction) in its own storage, for that file only. Nothing leaves your browser; clearing the site data removes it.</li>
        <li>Links in a deck (for example to a source on GitHub) open only when you click them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No analytics, no cookies, no advertising, no third-party scripts or fonts.</li>
        <li>Your browser stores your light/dark choice, and the configurator stores the form you fill in, so it's still there next time. Both stay in your browser; the configurator sends nothing anywhere.</li>
        <li>The GitHub star count comes from this site's own address (<code>/api/stars</code>), which asks GitHub at most about once an hour. Your browser never contacts GitHub for it.</li>
        <li>The site is hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes technical request data (such as IP addresses) to deliver the site and protect it from abuse, under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>. We don't receive or keep visitor logs.</li>
        <li>Downloads and install scripts come from GitHub, under <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or concerns: open an issue on <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}>GitHub</a> or write to
        {" "}<a href="mailto:hello@lukasvecerka.dev">hello@lukasvecerka.dev</a>. The software is provided under the
        {" "}<a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT License</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
